The Flow of Gifts

[Link]:
https://github.com/GoldenR1ver/AI-town

[Application scenarios]:
Social science and generative-agent research: testing an ethnographic claim about gift exchange by running it. Also a teaching demo: a 16-person, 10-act story replays in the browser without an API key.

[effectiveness]:
100 agents, 365 days, DeepSeek, seed=42: [1;33mreciprocity 0.90, default 0.022, average repay delay ~17 slots[0m. Gifts follow prestige (0.51) rather than money (0.07); prestige gini is 0.16 and the top 10 take 27% of gifts. All 344 dialogues passed the speech-state check. Turning off any one component degrades the phenomenon it carries.

[advantage]:
The LLM only talks. It proposes words and intentions; the RuleEngine alone writes money, gifts, debts and relationships, so every result can be settled, logged and replayed.

Each of the nine parts stands on published work, but the combination is mine: nine switchable mechanisms in one world, where a claim is tested by turning it off instead of tuning one good number. Three pairings work better than I expected.

- Cognitive tree × relationship graph. The edge is the objective relation; the tree is the relation as remembered. That split gives long-horizon continuity, and without it gifts drift one way up the status ladder.
- BDIE × Big Five. State explains the week (broke, stressed, a debt due), personality explains the person (introverted, picky, spiteful). Remove either and the village becomes an average agent.
- Event system × world engine. Events decide when something happens and who meets whom; the clock, state and settlement stay in the engine. Dialogue never moves either.

[method]:
Nine components in five layers. Each stands on a known framework and adds the one thing that framework cannot give.

Trigger
  8 Event system: public affairs, visits, gossip, repayment attempts. Discrete-event simulation; added, initiation is driven by personality and emotion, so timing bursts instead of running flat.

Expression
  6 Dialogue: two-person, host and group modes. Generative agents (Park et al.); added, the prompt uses the same variables as settlement and may not claim world changes.

Memory
  5 PET personal event table: each agent's own recent events with confidence. From episodic memory; added, propagation follows visibility, so agents remember different things.
  4 Cognitive tree: social, circle, relation, dialogue; summaries promoted by accumulated influence. From Park et al.'s memory stream, made hierarchical; added, promotion becomes subjective relationship memory and returns to later prompts.

Cognition
  3 BDIE: belief, desire, intention plus mood, arousal, stress and energy. From BDI (Rao and Georgeff) and OCC appraisal (Ortony et al.); added, a near-due gift debt is lifted to priority 95-99 and becomes repay urgency.
  9 Personality: Big Five mapped to introversion, pickiness and spitefulness. From Costa and McCrae; added, the three only set refusal and gossip probabilities, never the outcome.

Settlement
  1 RuleEngine + gift ledger: the only write gate, with pending, replied, defaulted and closed, and windows W4-W10. From institutional simulation and Yan Yunxiang's gift theory; added, the window and vertical exemption make delay and asymmetry emerge rather than be scripted.
  2 Relationship graph: directed edges carrying trust, intimacy, dislike and debt, plus a vertical axis and social basis. From signed network theory; added, the axis, which is the only carrier of vertical asymmetry.
  7 Economy: living cost, wages, savings and gift expectation checks. From budget-constrained agents; added, an affordability factor that turns "has money" into "can repay".
